Z||000000||FieroBUZZ||07-21-2001||08:15 PM||||I broke my antenna off (accidentally) and need some info on replacement.  As I sometimes use a car cover, the stock 'stick' won't do.<BR> I have room where the battery used to live for an electric antenna, but I have read a couple of references to a hidden amplified antenna which can be under dash or trunk mounted.  The local outlets have no idea what I mean and as far as I can tell don't realize cars have antennae at all.<P>The equipment involved is a Sony Xplod CD-Mp3 player with front 4x10's and rear 4x6's. Also attached are two Pioneer amps driving 4 and 6.5 inch speakers.<P>If someone has info on the antenna availability please LMK (Canada preferred)<BR>Thanks all,<BR>Gary||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||07-21-2001||08:22 PM||||Run a wire connected to the ant wire on your reciever along the backside of the a-pillar plastic. It is still verticle, so you should catch a pretty good signal still.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TRiAD||07-21-2001||09:37 PM||||Fierobuzz, I carry Zendar antennas, they have what I think you're talking about, as well as "fuba" style antennas, and "mini-racing" styles. They are generally inexpensive, and come with the cables required. <P>Email me for more info. <P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||07-21-2001||09:47 PM||||If you have a sunroof there's a stainless steel metal ring stamping in the frame that can be used for a excellent antenna. You just run your antenna wire up the right hand windsheild pillar under the interior trim and attach it to the stainless ring. The Michigan Fiero Club published an article on it (can't find it now), you may want to contact them or check their web site. Then all you have to do is fill the hole in the fender and paint it.||||reg||6
